From 5a882d6ecac2364c334daae695dea588daa3cf89 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenNVR <opennvr@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 18:10:10 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] rtsp: synchronize tracks with RTCP sender reports

The RTP time decoder aligns tracks once, when their first packet arrives,
and then advances every track with its own RTP clock. Many IP cameras
(observed on Uniview) generate video RTP timestamps from the nominal frame
rate (i.e. 9000 ticks per frame at 10fps) while the encoder actually runs
slightly faster or slower (-1663 ppm and +809 ppm measured), so video and
audio drift apart by seconds per hour. Once the drift exceeds 1s, the
recorder discards the lagging track ("sample of track N received too
late, discarding") and labels footage with a growing time shift
(see https://github.com/bluenviron/mediamtx/issues/6160).

Cameras declare the true relation between RTP timestamps and their
clock in RTCP sender reports. Use them to keep every track of a RTSP
session (static sources and publishers) aligned with a reference track
(the first audio track):

- the camera clock is only used to compute differences between tracks;
  it is never exposed, so session and recording times keep coming from
  the server clock;
- the reference track is never modified;
- before sender reports are available, or when they are never sent,
  timestamps are the ones of the RTP time decoder, as before;
- the offset between a track and the reference is estimated with a
  robust (Theil-Sen) line fit over 5 minutes, which filters the +-30ms
  quantization noise of sender reports and allows to extrapolate when
  reports go missing; drift up to 5% is followed;
- sudden jumps (i.e. a camera clock step seen by one track before the
  other) are ignored unless they persist for 30s;
- the correction changes by at most 10% of the elapsed media time and
  only when a track moves forward, so timestamps stay monotonic
  (B-frames too);
- the correction is kept within 500ms of the alignment given by arrival
  times, so tracks are never delivered with a skew the recorder can't
  handle, even if sender reports are wrong.
